Chapter: ListOobRead
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 An index past the end of a list must trap. Before this guard `list-at`
 scaled the index, added it to the base and loaded, with no check at either
 end, so a read past the end returned whatever the heap put there next.

 That is why the probe locates the neighbour by scanning rather than
 picking an index. Two earlier attempts wrote at index 8 and index 400 and
 saw nothing: 8 landed in capacity padding and 400 flew clean over the
 neighbour into unallocated heap. An out-of-bounds probe that finds nothing
 has usually just missed.

Section: Entry

  read-past : List Integer, List Integer -> Integer
  read-past (t) (marker) = list-at t (list-length t + 2)

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("got=" & show (read-past [1, 2, 3] [999, 999, 999]))
  end

